/**
\defgroup bijector_scalar Scalar bijectors

The scalar bijector module (`#include <laplace/bijector/scalar.hpp>`) provides twenty monotone one-dimensional maps with inverses, log-Jacobians, derivatives and parameter partials to order 6, non-smooth points, and bound distances. Parameter-free maps use `scalar_bijector{scalar_bijector_map::NAME}`, parameterized maps use static factories on `scalar_bijector`, and every member carries `LAPLACE_HD` for host and CUDA execution. Over orders 0 to 6 on gcc and CUDA, parameter-free maps stay within 11 ulpC and parameterized maps within 50 ulpC.

## Definitions

### Mathematical convention and inversion

A bijector defines a strictly monotone forward map \f$g : V \to U\f$, inverse \f$h = g^{-1} : U \to V\f$, and log-Jacobians \f$\log|g'(v)|\f$ and \f$\log|h'(u)|\f$. Passing `true` (`scalar_bijector{map, true}` or factory `inverted = true`) swaps forward and inverse: `forward` evaluates \f$h\f$, `inverse` evaluates \f$g\f$, the forward domain becomes \f$U\f$, and `is_decreasing()` is unchanged.

### Parameter-free maps

Here \f$\Phi\f$ is the standard normal cumulative distribution function and \f$\phi(z) = \frac{1}{\sqrt{2\pi}} e^{-z^2/2}\f$ its density. All twelve parameter-free maps are strictly increasing except `inverse` and `inverse_sq`, which are strictly decreasing.

| Map | \f$g(v)\f$ | \f$h(u)\f$ | \f$V\f$ | \f$U\f$ |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| `identity` | \f$v\f$ | \f$u\f$ | \f$(-\infty, \infty)\f$ | \f$(-\infty, \infty)\f$ |
| `log` | \f$\log v\f$ | \f$e^u\f$ | \f$(0, \infty)\f$ | \f$(-\infty, \infty)\f$ |
| `logit` | \f$\log(v / (1 - v))\f$ | \f$1 / (1 + e^{-u})\f$ | \f$(0, 1)\f$ | \f$(-\infty, \infty)\f$ |
| `probit` | \f$\Phi^{-1}(v)\f$ | \f$\Phi(u)\f$ | \f$(0, 1)\f$ | \f$(-\infty, \infty)\f$ |
| `cauchit` | \f$\tan(\pi(v - 1/2))\f$ | \f$\frac{1}{\pi}\mathrm{atan2}(1, -u)\f$ | \f$(0, 1)\f$ | \f$(-\infty, \infty)\f$ |
| `cloglog` | \f$\log(-\log(1 - v))\f$ | \f$1 - \exp(-e^u)\f$ | \f$(0, 1)\f$ | \f$(-\infty, \infty)\f$ |
| `loglog` | \f$-\log(-\log v)\f$ | \f$\exp(-e^{-u})\f$ | \f$(0, 1)\f$ | \f$(-\infty, \infty)\f$ |
| `inverse` | \f$1 / v\f$ | \f$1 / u\f$ | \f$(0, \infty)\f$ | \f$(0, \infty)\f$ |
| `inverse_sq` | \f$1 / v^2\f$ | \f$1 / \sqrt{u}\f$ | \f$(0, \infty)\f$ | \f$(0, \infty)\f$ |
| `sqrt` | \f$\sqrt{v}\f$ | \f$u^2\f$ | \f$(0, \infty)\f$ | \f$(0, \infty)\f$ |
| `atanh` | \f$\frac{1}{2}\log((1 + v)/(1 - v))\f$ | \f$\tanh u\f$ | \f$(-1, 1)\f$ | \f$(-\infty, \infty)\f$ |
| `asinh` | \f$\mathrm{asinh}(v)\f$ | \f$\sinh u\f$ | \f$(-\infty, \infty)\f$ | \f$(-\infty, \infty)\f$ |

The `atanh` map is also known as Fisher's \f$z\f$ transformation (1915).

### Maps with parameters

Eight parameterized maps are constructed via static factories on `scalar_bijector`. All are strictly increasing except `affine` with \f$\sigma < 0\f$ and `power` with \f$\lambda < 0\f$, which are strictly decreasing.

| Map | \f$g(v)\f$ | \f$h(u)\f$ | \f$V\f$ | \f$U\f$ | Parameters |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| `affine` | \f$(v - \mu) / \sigma\f$ | \f$\mu + \sigma u\f$ | \f$(-\infty, \infty)\f$ | \f$(-\infty, \infty)\f$ | location \f$\mu \in (-\infty, \infty)\f$, scale \f$\sigma \ne 0\f$ |
| `power` | \f$v^\lambda\f$ | \f$u^{1/\lambda}\f$ | \f$(0, \infty)\f$ | \f$(0, \infty)\f$ | exponent \f$\lambda \ne 0\f$ |
| `softplus` | \f$\frac{1}{a}\log(e^{a v} - 1)\f$ | \f$\frac{1}{a}\log(1 + e^{a u})\f$ | \f$(0, \infty)\f$ | \f$(-\infty, \infty)\f$ | sharpness \f$a > 0\f$ |
| `bounded` | \f$\log(v - a) - \log(b - v)\f$ | \f$a + (b - a)/(1 + e^{-u})\f$ | \f$(a, b)\f$ | \f$(-\infty, \infty)\f$ | lower \f$a\f$, upper \f$b\f$ (\f$a < b\f$) |
| `lower_bounded` | \f$\log(v - a)\f$ | \f$a + e^u\f$ | \f$(a, \infty)\f$ | \f$(-\infty, \infty)\f$ | lower \f$a\f$ |
| `upper_bounded` | \f$-\log(b - v)\f$ | \f$b - e^{-u}\f$ | \f$(-\infty, b)\f$ | \f$(-\infty, \infty)\f$ | upper \f$b\f$ |
| `box_cox` | \f$(v^\lambda - 1)/\lambda\f$ (\f$\log v\f$ at \f$\lambda = 0\f$) | \f$(1 + \lambda u)^{1/\lambda}\f$ (\f$e^u\f$ at \f$\lambda = 0\f$) | \f$(0, \infty)\f$ | \f$(-1/\lambda, \infty)\f$ (\f$\lambda > 0\f$), \f$(-\infty, -1/\lambda)\f$ (\f$\lambda < 0\f$), \f$(-\infty, \infty)\f$ (\f$\lambda = 0\f$) | exponent \f$\lambda \in (-\infty, \infty)\f$ |
| `yeo_johnson` | \f$((1 + v)^\lambda - 1)/\lambda\f$ (\f$v \ge 0\f$), \f$-((1 - v)^{2 - \lambda} - 1)/(2 - \lambda)\f$ (\f$v < 0\f$) | \f$(1 + \lambda u)^{1/\lambda} - 1\f$ (\f$u \ge 0\f$), \f$1 - (1 - (2 - \lambda) u)^{1/(2 - \lambda)}\f$ (\f$u < 0\f$) | \f$(-\infty, \infty)\f$ | \f$(-\infty, \infty)\f$ (\f$0 \le \lambda \le 2\f$), \f$(-\infty, -1/\lambda)\f$ (\f$\lambda < 0\f$), \f$(1/(2 - \lambda), \infty)\f$ (\f$\lambda > 2\f$) | exponent \f$\lambda \in (-\infty, \infty)\f$ |

Calling `scalar_bijector::power(exponent)` with `1`, `0.5`, `-1`, `-2`, or `0` returns `identity`, `sqrt`, `inverse`, `inverse_sq`, or `log`.

### Parameter partial derivatives

For total order \f$K \in \{1, \dots, 6\}\f$, \ref laplace::bijector::bijector_partials "bijector_partials" stores `double c[(K + 1) * (K + 2) / 2]` for partials of orders \f$n_0\f$ (argument), \f$n_1\f$ (\f$p_1\f$), and \f$n_2\f$ (\f$p_2\f$) with \f$n_0 + n_1 + n_2 = K\f$. Entry \f$(n_1, n_2)\f$ lies at \ref laplace::bijector::bijector_partials::index "index"(\f$n_1, n_2\f$) \f$= n_2(K + 1) - n_2(n_2 - 1)/2 + n_1\f$, with the pure argument derivative at `c[0]`. `parameter_1()`, `parameter_2()`, and `parameter_count()` return active parameter values (`0.0` when absent) and count (`0`, `1`, or `2`). Parameter-free maps zero `c[1 ... ]`; `power` fills `c[1 ... ]` with quiet NaN because \f$\lambda\f$-derivatives diverge as \f$\lambda \to 0\f$.

### Non-smooth points

\ref laplace::bijector::bijector_kinks "bijector_kinks", returned by `forward_kinks()` and `inverse_kinks()`, reports `count`, `point`, and `continuous_order`. Only `yeo_johnson` with \f$\lambda \ne 1\f$ has a kink, at \f$0\f$ (`count = 1`, `continuous_order = 2`, third-derivative jump \f$(1 - \lambda)(2 - \lambda)\f$), using the right-hand branch (\f$v \ge 0\f$, \f$u \ge 0\f$). Otherwise `count` is `0`.

### Distances to the bounds

`inverse_distance_upper(u)` and `inverse_distance_lower(u)` evaluate \f$v_{\max} - h(u)\f$ and \f$h(u) - v_{\min}\f$ directly for bounded and shifted exponential maps without rounding-induced cancellation:

| Map | Upper distance \f$v_{\max} - h(u)\f$ | Lower distance \f$h(u) - v_{\min}\f$ |
|---|---|---|
| `logit` | \f$\mathrm{expit}(-u)\f$ | \f$h(u)\f$ |
| `probit` | \f$\Phi(-u)\f$ | \f$h(u)\f$ |
| `cauchit` | \f$h(-u)\f$ | \f$h(u)\f$ |
| `cloglog` | \f$\exp(-e^u)\f$ | \f$h(u)\f$ |
| `loglog` | \f$1 - \exp(-e^{-u})\f$ | \f$h(u)\f$ |
| `atanh` | \f$2e/(1 + e)\f$ (\f$e = e^{-2u}\f$, \f$u > 0\f$); \f$1 - \tanh u\f$ (\f$u \le 0\f$) | \f$2e/(1 + e)\f$ (\f$e = e^{2u}\f$, \f$u < 0\f$); \f$1 + \tanh u\f$ (\f$u \ge 0\f$) |
| `bounded` | \f$(b - a)\operatorname{expit}(-u)\f$ | \f$(b - a)\operatorname{expit}(u)\f$ |
| `lower_bounded` | \f$+\infty\f$ | \f$e^u\f$ |
| `upper_bounded` | \f$e^{-u}\f$ | \f$+\infty\f$ |

The other maps, and inverted bijectors in either direction, take the difference itself. At an end of the closed domain of \f$h\f$ the difference is taken in the limit, so it is zero where \f$h(u)\f$ reaches the bound from which the distance is measured, the width \f$v_{\max} - v_{\min}\f$ where it reaches the other bound and both bounds are finite, and \f$+\infty\f$ where it reaches the other bound and that one is infinite. A bound that is itself infinite gives \f$+\infty\f$ at every point of the closed domain, the end included, where \f$h(u)\f$ is that same infinity.

## Domain and special values

Inputs strictly outside the closed domain or NaN yield quiet NaN. Invalid parameters (non-finite values, scale \f$0\f$, exponent \f$0\f$ in `power`, sharpness \f$\le 0\f$, or \f$a \ge b\f$ in `bounded`) likewise make bounds and evaluations return quiet NaN. Domain boundaries yield mathematical limits such as \f$\mathrm{logit}(0) = -\infty\f$ and \f$\mathrm{logit}(1) = +\infty\f$. For `box_cox` and `yeo_johnson` that holds throughout, for the value, for the derivatives in the argument, for the log-Jacobian with its own argument derivatives and for the derivatives in \f$\lambda\f$, and for those two maps an infinite argument is itself such a boundary. The forward domain of `box_cox` is \f$(0, \infty)\f$, so \f$v = 0\f$ and \f$v = +\infty\f$ are both closed ends, and the forward domain of `yeo_johnson` is the whole line, so \f$v = \pm\infty\f$ are its two ends. The forward map therefore returns an end of its image at an infinite argument, and the inverse map returns an end of its range at an end of its domain. Where a derivative of order \f$n\f$ is governed by a power of the argument, it takes a finite limit at the value of \f$\lambda\f$ where that power's exponent vanishes, and an infinity on one side of that value with zero on the other. Values are never clamped; overflow and underflow follow IEEE double precision.

## Method

Every direction is evaluated directly rather than numerically inverted, with derivative polynomials derived in SymPy 1.14.0 (Meurer and others, 2017). `identity`, `log`, `inverse`, `inverse_sq`, and `sqrt` use closed-form exponential and monomial derivatives.

`logit` and `atanh` factor even-order forward derivatives via \f$S_k(a, b) = \sum_{j=0}^{k-1} a^{k-1-j} b^j\f$ (\f$a = 1/v\f$, \f$b = 1/(1 - v)\f$ for `logit`). Inverse `logit` sets \f$e = e^{-|u|}\f$, \f$U = e/(1 + e)^2\f$, and \f$D = 1 - 2p\f$ (\f$p = h(u)\f$) for \f$h^{(k)}(u) = U(A_k(U) + D B_k(U))\f$ from \f$h'(u) = U\f$, \f$U' = U D\f$, \f$D' = -2U\f$. Inverse `atanh` sets \f$T = \tanh u\f$ and \f$S = 1 - T^2\f$ for \f$S P_k(T)\f$ via \f$P_1 = 1\f$, \f$P_{k+1} = (1 - T^2)P_k' - 2T P_k\f$ expanded in \f$T^2\f$ when \f$T^2 < 1/2\f$ and in \f$S\f$ otherwise. `bounded` mirrors `logit`; `lower_bounded` and `upper_bounded` shift `log` with \f$\partial/\partial p_1 = -\partial/\partial v\f$.

`probit` evaluates \f$1/\phi(z)\f$ at \f$z = \Phi^{-1}(v)\f$ from the Mills ratio (Mills, 1926) \f$m(z) = \Phi(z)/\phi(z) = \sqrt{\pi/2}\operatorname{erfcx}(-z/\sqrt{2})\f$ as \f$m(z)/v\f$ for \f$v \le 1/2\f$ and \f$\sqrt{\pi/2}\operatorname{erfcx}(z/\sqrt{2})/(1 - v)\f$ above. Forward derivatives are \f$Q_k(z)/\phi(z)^k\f$ (\f$Q_1 = 1\f$, \f$Q_{k+1} = Q_k' + k z Q_k\f$), log-Jacobian derivatives are \f$R_k(z)/\phi(z)^k\f$ (\f$R_1 = z\f$, \f$R_{k+1} = R_k' + k z R_k\f$), and inverse derivatives are \f$(-1)^{k-1}\mathrm{He}_{k-1}(u)\phi(u)\f$.

`cauchit` (Koenker and Yoon, 2009) evaluates \f$\tan(\pi(v - 1/2))\f$ as \f$-1/\tan(\pi v)\f$ for \f$v < 1/4\f$ and as \f$1/\tan(\pi(1 - v))\f$ for \f$v > 3/4\f$, with forward derivatives \f$\pi^k T_k(z)\f$ (\f$T_1 = 1 + z^2\f$, \f$T_{k+1} = (1 + z^2) T_k'\f$). With \f$r = 1/\operatorname{hypot}(1, x)\f$, \f$s = x r\f$ and \f$r^2 = 1 - s^2\f$, and the expansion in \f$s^2\f$ when \f$s^2 < 1/2\f$ and in \f$r^2\f$ otherwise, `cauchit` inverse uses \f$W_{m+1} = -(m + 2)s W_m + (1 - s^2) W_m'\f$ (\f$W_0 = 1\f$), `asinh` forward uses \f$V_{m+1} = -(m + 1)s V_m + (1 - s^2) V_m'\f$ (\f$V_0 = 1\f$), and both use \f$X_{m+1} = -(m + 1)s X_m + (1 - s^2) X_m'\f$ (\f$X_0 = s\f$) for log-Jacobians.

`cloglog` (McCullagh and Nelder, 1989) sets \f$t = \mathrm{log1p}(-v)\f$ and \f$c = 1/((1 - v) t)\f$, giving \f$g^{(k)}(v) = (-1)^k C_k(t) c^k\f$ (\f$C_1 = 1\f$, \f$C_{k+1} = t C_k' - k(1 + t) C_k\f$) and \f$E_k(t) = -C_k(t) + (-1)^k(k - 1)! t^k\f$ for the log-Jacobian. Its inverse uses \f$w = e^u\f$ (\f$0.0\f$ for higher derivatives when \f$w > 1000\f$) via \f$P_1(w) = 1\f$, \f$P_{k+1}(w) = (1 - w) P_k(w) + w P_k'(w)\f$, and \f$P_2(w) = -\mathrm{expm1}(u)\f$. `loglog` sets \f$t = \log v\f$ with \f$C_k\f$ and \f$E_k\f$, and inverse \f$h(u) = 1 - h_{\mathrm{cloglog}}(-u)\f$.

`power` splits \f$\lambda - k\f$ and \f$1/\lambda - k\f$ via Knuth's two-sum algorithm (1997) \f$s + e\f$, computing \f$x^s(1 + e\log x)\f$ and \f$1/\lambda - j = \mathrm{fma}(-j, \lambda, 1)/\lambda\f$. `softplus` (Dugas and others, 2001) uses a Bernoulli series (DLMF §24.2) of \f$-\log\operatorname{exprel}(-a v)\f$ for mixed forward log-Jacobian partials on \f$0 < a v < 3\f$ and \f$-\log(1 - e^{-a v})\f$ beyond. `box_cox` (Box and Cox, 1964) and `yeo_johnson` (Yeo, 2000) use \f$\operatorname{exprel}\f$ and \f$\operatorname{log1prel}\f$ (switching `box_cox` forward to \f$(v^\lambda - 1)/\lambda\f$ for \f$|\lambda\log v| \ge 1\f$), assembling inverse partials via partial Bell polynomials (Comtet, 1974).

## Accuracy

Errors use the \ref accuracy_conditioned "conditioned scale" (\ref accuracy "How accuracy is measured"): \f$s = |f_k(x)| + |x f_{k+1}(x)|\f$ for parameter-free functions (using \f$h'\f$ as \f$f_{k+1}\f$ for bound distances) and \f$s = |f| + |x\,\partial f/\partial x| + |p_1\,\partial f/\partial p_1| + |p_2\,\partial f/\partial p_2|\f$ for parameterized functions. Maximum ulpC over orders 0 to 6 (`gcc / CUDA`):

| Group | Max error (gcc / CUDA) | Worst quantity |
|---|---|---|
| Parameter-free maps | 11 / 10.9 | `loglog` inverse (gcc), `sqrt` forward (CUDA) |
| Parameter-free distances | 2.28 / 2.28 | six bounded parameter-free maps |
| `affine`, `power`, `bounded`, `lower_bounded`, `upper_bounded` | 7.09 / 5.88 | `bounded` inverse (gcc), `power` forward log-Jacobian (CUDA) |
| `softplus`, `box_cox`, `yeo_johnson` | 18.4 / 18.4; 42.8 / 46.3; 27.8 / 30.5 | `softplus` order-6 forward log-Jacobian (\f$a v = 7.6\f$); `box_cox`, `yeo_johnson` order 4–6 inverses (\f$\lambda = 0\f$) |

`identity` is exact (`0` ulpC); outside higher-order `box_cox`, `yeo_johnson`, and `softplus` peaks, remaining parameterized quantities stay within 8 ulpC on both builds, except the forward map of `box_cox` (7.97 ulpC on gcc, 9.71 on CUDA). See `docs/numerics.md`.

## Speed

Over \f$2^{16}\f$ interior points on one gcc thread, scalar evaluations and log-Jacobians take 3–70 ns, order-1 partials 5–78 ns, and order-6 partials 15–116 ns (193–268 ns for `yeo_johnson` and `box_cox`). With six OpenMP threads, timings fall to 0.9–14.3 ns for most maps and 37.8–45.8 ns for order-6 partials of `yeo_johnson` and `box_cox`. On CUDA (NVIDIA RTX 4070 SUPER), calls take 0.38–3.64 ns except `box_cox` and `yeo_johnson` partials (8.34–11.2 ns at order 1, 27.4–27.8 ns at order 6).

## Example

Evaluating \f$h(u)\f$ and \f$h'(u)\f$ for `logit` inside a `Kokkos::parallel_for` kernel:

\code{.cpp}
#include <laplace/bijector/scalar.hpp>
#include <Kokkos_Core.hpp>

void evaluate_expit(const Kokkos::View<const double*>& u,
                    Kokkos::View<double*>& h,
                    Kokkos::View<double*>& dh,
                    int n) {
  const laplace::bijector::scalar_bijector b{laplace::bijector::scalar_bijector_map::logit};
  Kokkos::parallel_for("evaluate_expit", n, KOKKOS_LAMBDA(const int i) {
    h(i)  = b.inverse(u(i));
    dh(i) = b.inverse_d1(u(i));
  });
}
\endcode

## Module functions

All member functions belong to \ref laplace::bijector::scalar_bijector "scalar_bijector".

- Types: \ref laplace::bijector::scalar_bijector_map "scalar_bijector_map", \ref laplace::bijector::scalar_bijector "scalar_bijector", \ref laplace::bijector::bijector_partials "bijector_partials" (with \ref laplace::bijector::bijector_partials::index "index()"), and \ref laplace::bijector::bijector_kinks "bijector_kinks".
- Factories for maps with parameters: \ref laplace::bijector::scalar_bijector::affine "affine()", \ref laplace::bijector::scalar_bijector::power "power()", \ref laplace::bijector::scalar_bijector::softplus "softplus()", \ref laplace::bijector::scalar_bijector::bounded "bounded()", \ref laplace::bijector::scalar_bijector::lower_bounded "lower_bounded()", \ref laplace::bijector::scalar_bijector::upper_bounded "upper_bounded()", \ref laplace::bijector::scalar_bijector::box_cox "box_cox()", and \ref laplace::bijector::scalar_bijector::yeo_johnson "yeo_johnson()".
- Parameter inspection: \ref laplace::bijector::scalar_bijector::parameter_1 "parameter_1()", \ref laplace::bijector::scalar_bijector::parameter_2 "parameter_2()", and \ref laplace::bijector::scalar_bijector::parameter_count "parameter_count()".
- Forward value and derivatives: \ref laplace::bijector::scalar_bijector::forward "forward()", \ref laplace::bijector::scalar_bijector::forward_d1 "forward_d1()", \ref laplace::bijector::scalar_bijector::forward_d2 "forward_d2()", \ref laplace::bijector::scalar_bijector::forward_d3 "forward_d3()", \ref laplace::bijector::scalar_bijector::forward_d4 "forward_d4()", \ref laplace::bijector::scalar_bijector::forward_d5 "forward_d5()", and \ref laplace::bijector::scalar_bijector::forward_d6 "forward_d6()".
- Inverse value and derivatives: \ref laplace::bijector::scalar_bijector::inverse "inverse()", \ref laplace::bijector::scalar_bijector::inverse_d1 "inverse_d1()", \ref laplace::bijector::scalar_bijector::inverse_d2 "inverse_d2()", \ref laplace::bijector::scalar_bijector::inverse_d3 "inverse_d3()", \ref laplace::bijector::scalar_bijector::inverse_d4 "inverse_d4()", \ref laplace::bijector::scalar_bijector::inverse_d5 "inverse_d5()", and \ref laplace::bijector::scalar_bijector::inverse_d6 "inverse_d6()".
- Forward log-Jacobian and derivatives: \ref laplace::bijector::scalar_bijector::forward_log_jacobian "forward_log_jacobian()", \ref laplace::bijector::scalar_bijector::forward_log_jacobian_d1 "forward_log_jacobian_d1()", \ref laplace::bijector::scalar_bijector::forward_log_jacobian_d2 "forward_log_jacobian_d2()", \ref laplace::bijector::scalar_bijector::forward_log_jacobian_d3 "forward_log_jacobian_d3()", \ref laplace::bijector::scalar_bijector::forward_log_jacobian_d4 "forward_log_jacobian_d4()", \ref laplace::bijector::scalar_bijector::forward_log_jacobian_d5 "forward_log_jacobian_d5()", and \ref laplace::bijector::scalar_bijector::forward_log_jacobian_d6 "forward_log_jacobian_d6()".
- Inverse log-Jacobian and derivatives: \ref laplace::bijector::scalar_bijector::inverse_log_jacobian "inverse_log_jacobian()", \ref laplace::bijector::scalar_bijector::inverse_log_jacobian_d1 "inverse_log_jacobian_d1()", \ref laplace::bijector::scalar_bijector::inverse_log_jacobian_d2 "inverse_log_jacobian_d2()", \ref laplace::bijector::scalar_bijector::inverse_log_jacobian_d3 "inverse_log_jacobian_d3()", \ref laplace::bijector::scalar_bijector::inverse_log_jacobian_d4 "inverse_log_jacobian_d4()", \ref laplace::bijector::scalar_bijector::inverse_log_jacobian_d5 "inverse_log_jacobian_d5()", and \ref laplace::bijector::scalar_bijector::inverse_log_jacobian_d6 "inverse_log_jacobian_d6()".
